Since $\angle DEC$ is twice $a=\angle ADE$ and the sum of the interior angles of triangle $DEC$ is $180^{\circ}$, then $a+\angle DEC + \angle CDE = 180^{\circ}$.  Substituting for $\angle CDE$ and $\angle DEC$, we have
\begin{align*}
&{}a + \angle BDE + 2a = 180^{\circ} \\
&\Rightarrow 3a + \angle BDE = 180^{\circ} \\
&\Rightarrow \angleDEB = 2(180^{\circ} - 3a)
\end{align*}But triangles $BDE$ and $CDA$ are similar, so the ratios of their corresponding sides are equal.  This implies
\[\frac{DE}{BC} = \frac{BD}{CA}.\]But we also know that $BD=\frac12 BC$ and $CA=\frac12 DE$ and so the triangles are similar; therefore
\[\frac{DE}{BC}.\]From the equation $\angle DEB = 2(180^{\circ} - 3a)$, we can see that a given amount of angle $a$ will create a greater angle at vertex $B$; thus, a smaller angle at $E$.  Solving for $a$, we get $90^{\circ}=2a\Longrightarrow 45^{\circ}=a$.

